7] [17592186048422 #edb/ref 1031 "The Distributive Law for Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048422 #edb/ref 1033 349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048422 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "9dd30f5e-90f1-5ed7-bd60-9c0dea37e1e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1031 "Adding and Subtracting Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1033 81 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048524 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a6b1981e-1258-53df-8262-f14fa4fe97b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1031 "Simplifying Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1033 1783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049538 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fcf84897-2da1-577a-877e-c8fd8d1350aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1031 "Collecting Like Terms in Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1033 134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050452 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4a33a1d4-8dfa-5e25-8ed4-4aa1496ac8a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119042 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1ba7977f-c9de-5cb6-8c79-d9ded44d72b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119529 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119529 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $x^3+x-2x^2-3x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "473d3810-3c45-5e76-87a2-9cc4d194a2b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119529 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, $x$ and $-3x$ are like terms, so we group them, and then we add or subtract the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x-2{x}^{2}-3x & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+x-3x-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+(x-3x)-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+(1-3)x-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}-2x-2{x}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119530 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119530 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite the polynomial below in standard form. \n\n$$\n8x-9-3x^4 - 6x^3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f65e4129-1564-52d2-80b0-5c2e970722e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119530 #edb/ref 1074 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms in descending order of the variable's exponent. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $3x^4,$ followed by $-6x^3,$ and then $8x,$ and finally $-9.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n-3x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x-9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119531 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119531 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $3x^2-2-4x - 6x^2+18,$ giving your final answer in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "409cbc8a-e56e-587b-a639-ecb2c2c0cc97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119531 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-2-4x-6{x}^{2}+18 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}-2+18-4x & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2})+(-2+18)-4x & = \\\\ (3-6){x}^{2}+(-2+18)-4x & = \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+16-4x & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to express our polynomial in standard form. \n\nThe highest exponent term is $-3x^2,$ followed by $-4x,$ and finally $16.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n-3x^2-4x+16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119768 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b66305f-f020-5236-a344-502bc54f8cc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Polynomials in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119769 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "af3f4c35-be23-5a1a-97fb-18e89ab51591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying and Writing Polynomials in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119770 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6f00d5b6-5c41-5499-a897-b3ea7cbb3432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1045 "Simplifying Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120005 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af23758d-7476-5d58-8b4e-e0d2c3022048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1071 "The standard form of the polynomial $2+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07524f9b-4a87-5a60-baad-877be227b03b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120488 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & = \\\\ 2+(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2})+(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ 2+(3-1){x}^{2}+(4-1){x}^{3} & = \\\\ 2+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to express our polynomial in standard form.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $3{x}^{3},$ followed by $2{x}^{2},$ and finally $2.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is:\n\n$$\n3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1061 150900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8789d5b3-8f11-513e-a769-fad4e067d79a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1061 150901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2339f28a-1bfe-5d7f-a1d4-4457e0b9dd39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1061 150904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab63d222-6bc9-5825-a10a-531c4978485f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1061 150905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "357f8d99-13be-51ca-a9b3-f2b34dc03d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1061 150908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8288291c-78f5-5a1b-99f9-23e99b20eada" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120617 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5\n$$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+5 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3-2){x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1065 8864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dcac1959-d802-5641-8736-12d53db273a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121146 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1064 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms of the variable's exponent in descending order. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nFor example, consider the following polynomial:\n\n$$\n6x-2x^2-2+x^3\n$$\n\nTo write this polynomial in standard form, we follow the steps below:\n\n1\\. Identify the term with the highest exponent ($x^3$) and write it first.\n\n2\\. Identify the term with the next highest exponent ($-2x^2$) and write it second.\n\n3\\. Continue identifying the term with the next highest exponent and writing it next until all of the polynomial terms have been written.\n\nTherefore, the standard form of our polynomial is\n\n$$\nx^3 -2x^2 + 6x -2.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The leading term always appears first when a polynomial is written in standard form. This is where the leading term gets its name!\n\n- When a polynomial is written in standard form, we also say that the terms are written in **decreasing powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** or **descending powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** because the powers are decreasing/descending.\n\n- If we were to write the terms of our polynomial in *increasing* (or *ascending*) powers of $x,$ it would look as follows:\n\n$$\n-2 + 6x -2x^2 + x^3\n$$\n\nIn this case, the powers of $x$ *increase* as we read from left to right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1065 8868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40e8c8a9-715e-5a33-92e4-34369c27b505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121147 #edb/ref 1067 "Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476219 #edb/ref 1080 "2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0997ff7b-e53b-5695-bfa9-fe2333cddfff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476220 #edb/ref 1080 "-6{w}^{9}+3{w}^{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476220 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476220 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aab9ce2-cbfa-5ebd-884c-a4950519a3af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476234 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476234 #